    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d542">

                <pb facs="1294" n="1180" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg1180"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/8–2643: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Foreign Office to the British Ambassador in
                        Portugal</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Campbell</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d542-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">most secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-26">August 26,
                        1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>You should communicate comprehensive text to General Z. with the explanation that
                    it embodies both the short terms given General C. and political and economic
                    terms which General G. was warned to expect<anchor n="2" xml:id="d542-f2"/> (see
                    Article 12 of short terms). You should tell him that these are the terms which
                    General Eisenhower will present to any Italian emissary who presents himself in
                    the event of the Italian Government deciding to surrender. It should be pointed
                    out to him that it would obviously be more convenient that surrender should take
                    the form of signature straight away of this comprehensive document rather than
                    signature at different times of the two documents, i.e., the short <pb
                        facs="1295" n="1181" xml:id="pg1181"/> terms communicated to General C. and
                    at a later date the comprehensive document.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d542-f3"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d542-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d542-f1-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy made available to the Department of State by the British
                    Embassy at Washington.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d542-f2" n="2" xml:id="d542-f2-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg566">p. 566</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d542-f3" n="3" xml:id="d542-f3-content">
                <p>In accordance with these instructions, Campbell gave Zanussi the text of the
                    “long” or “comprehensive” terms of surrender on the morning of August 27, 1943.
                    See Garland and Smyth, p. 461.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
